1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,380
For my presentation today I want to tell you about how groups of elephants have been moved

2
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:09,550
and settled in new reserves.

3
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:12,500
This is known as translocation

4
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:17,670
and has been carried out in Malawi in Africa in recent years.

5
00:00:17,670 --> 00:00:20,060
The reason this is being done is

6
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:25,260
because of overpopulation of elephants in some areas.

7
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:29,420
Overpopulation is a good problem to have

8
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:32,740
and not one we tend to hear about very often.

9
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:36,420
In Malawi's Majete National Park

10
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:40,076
the elephant population had been wiped out by poachers,

11
00:00:40,076 --> 00:00:43,500
who killed the elephants for their ivory.

12
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:51,840
But in 2003, the park was restocked and effective law enforcement was introduced.

13
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:56,680
Since then, not a single elephant has been poached.

14
00:00:56,680 --> 00:01:02,200
In this safe environment, the elephant population boomed.

15
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:09,250
Breeding went so well that there were more elephants than the park could support.

16
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:12,570
This led to a number of problems.

17
00:01:12,570 --> 00:01:16,760
Firstly, there was more competition for food

18
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:21,830
which meant that some elephants were suffering from hunger.

19
00:01:21,830 --> 00:01:26,680
As there was a limit to the amount of food in the national park

20
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:30,550
some elephants began looking further afield.

21
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:36,073
Elephants were routinely knocking down fences around the park,

22
00:01:36,073 --> 00:01:42,210
which then had to be repaired at a significant cost.

23
00:01:42,210 --> 00:01:53,114
To solve this problem, the decision was made to move dozens of elephants from Majete National Park to Nkhotakota Wildlife Park,

24
00:01:53,114 --> 00:01:55,710
where there were no elephants.

25
00:01:55,710 --> 00:02:10,580
But, obviously, attempting to move significant numbers of elephants to a new home 300 kilometres away is quite a challenge.

26
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:16,870
So how did this translocation process work in practice?

27
00:02:16,870 --> 00:02:25,480
Elephants were moved in groups of between eight and twenty, all belonging to one family.

28
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:30,020
Because relationships are very important to elephants

29
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:33,700
they all had to be moved at the same time.

30
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:42,722
A team of vets and park rangers flew over the park in helicopters and targeted a group,

31
00:02:42,722 --> 00:02:48,560
which were rounded up and directed to a designated open plain.

32
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:55,215
The vets then used darts to immobilise the elephants - this was a tricky manoeuvre,

33
00:02:55,215 --> 00:03:02,346
as they not only had to select the right dose of tranquiliser for different-sized elephants

34
00:03:02,346 --> 00:03:08,050
but they had to dart the elephants as they were running around.

35
00:03:08,050 --> 00:03:17,120
This also had to be done as quickly as possible so as to minimise the stress caused.

36
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:21,180
As soon as the elephants began to flop onto the ground

37
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:24,900
the team moved in to take care of them.

38
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:27,460
To avoid the risk of suffocation

39
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:33,587
the team had to make sure none of the elephants were lying on their chests

40
00:03:33,587 --> 00:03:37,310
because their lungs could be crushed in this position.

41
00:03:37,310 --> 00:03:43,000
So all the elephants had to be placed on their sides.

42
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:50,220
One person stayed with each elephant while they waited for the vets to do checks.

43
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:55,793
It was very important to keep an eye on their breathing -

44
00:03:55,793 --> 00:03:59,380
if there were fewer than six breaths per minute

45
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:03,900
the elephant would need urgent medical attention.

46
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:07,860
Collars were fitted to the matriarch in each group

47
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:12,060
so their movements could be tracked in their new home.

48
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:16,517
Measurements were taken of each elephant's tusks -

49
00:04:16,517 --> 00:04:22,360
elephants with large tusks would be at greater risk from poachers

50
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:25,430
and also of their feet.

51
00:04:25,430 --> 00:04:32,633
The elephants were then taken to a recovery area before being loaded onto trucks

52
00:04:32,633 --> 00:04:35,720
and transported to their new home.

53
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:40,609
The elephants translocated to Nkhotakota settled in very well

54
00:04:40,609 --> 00:04:47,240
and the project has generally been accepted to have been a huge success

55
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:50,930
and not just for the elephants.

56
00:04:50,930 --> 00:04:55,700
Employment prospects have improved enormously

57
00:04:56,120 --> 00:05:00,760
contributing to rising living standards for the whole community.

58
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:03,720
Poaching is no longer an issue

59
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:09,710
as former poachers are able to find more reliable sources of income.

60
00:05:09,710 --> 00:05:13,920
In fact, many of them volunteered to give up their weapons

61
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:17,920
as they were no longer of any use to them.

62
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:25,330
More than two dozen elephants have been born at Nkhotakota since relocation.

63
00:05:25,330 --> 00:05:29,543
With an area of more than 1,800 square kilometres,

64
00:05:29,543 --> 00:05:35,020
there's plenty of space for the elephant population to continue to grow.

65
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:43,898
Their presence is also helping to rebalance Nkhotakota's damaged ecosystem

66
00:05:43,898 --> 00:05:45,600
and providing a sustainable conservation model

67
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:54,390
which could be replicated in other parks.

68
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:57,335
All this has been a big draw for tourism,

69
00:05:57,335 --> 00:06:01,262
which contributes five times more than the illegal wildlife trade to GDP,

70
00:06:01,262 --> 00:06:03,880
and this is mainly because of the elephants.

71
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:08,370
There's also been a dramatic rise in interest...
